  "live_records": {
    "q-84803": {
      "dom_id": "step-q84803",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A fair $12$-sided die with numbers $1$ through $12$ is rolled $20$ times. What is the probability that we get a number greater than $10$ more than $2$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times that a number greater than $10$ is rolled.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ rolls\n\n- the probability of getting a number greater than $10$ on any roll of the  $12$-sided die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{12}=\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,\\frac{1}{6}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x}){(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})}^{20-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x}){(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{20-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., a number greater than $10$) on more than $2$ rolls. This corresponds to $P(X>2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X>2)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=20)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{0}){(\\frac{1}{6})}^{0}{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{20-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{1}){(\\frac{1}{6})}^{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{20-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{2}){(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{20-2}] \\\\  & =1-[{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{20}+20(\\frac{1}{6}){(\\frac{5}{6})}^{19}+190{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{18}] \\\\  & =1-0.328\\,659 \\\\  & =0.671\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
