      "problem": "The probability that John succeeds in selling an insurance policy to a given customer is $0.45.$ On a given day, he interacts with $8$ customers. Assuming that the transactions with customers are independent, what is the probability that he will sell at least $6$ insurance policies on this day? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of insurance policies that John sells on this day.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ customers\n\n- the probability that John succeeds in selling an insurance policy to a given customer is $p=0.45$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.45).$  \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(0.45)}^{x}{(1-0.45)}^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(0.45)}^{x}{(0.55)}^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find  the probability  that John will sell at least $6$ insurance policies on this day. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =P(X=6)+P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{6}){(0.45)}^{6}{(0.55)}^{8-6}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7}){(0.45)}^{7}{(0.55)}^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8}){(0.45)}^{8}{(0.55)}^{8-8} \\\\  & =28{(0.45)}^{6}{(0.55)}^{2}+8{(0.45)}^{7}{(0.55)}^{1}+1{(0.45)}^{8}{(0.55)}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.088\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
