    "q-331489": {
      "dom_id": "step-q331489",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "A fair six-sided die is rolled $6$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting a $6$ exactly three times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times that a $6$ is rolled. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=6$ rolls.\n\n- The probability of getting a $6$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,\\frac{1}{6}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=3$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}){(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})}^{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{3!(6-3)!}{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3} \\\\  & =20{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.054,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
