    "q-331526": {
      "dom_id": "step-q331526",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "A new software program fails to run with probability $70%.$ If it is tested on $5$ computers, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that it fails on $1$ or fewer of them?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of failed runs.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ tests.\n\n- The probability of failure is $p=70%=0.7.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.7).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that $1$ or fewer of the $5$ software program runs fail. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.7)^{0}(0.3)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.7)^{1}(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =1(0.3)^{5}+5(0.7)(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.031,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
